WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.482
A new pod joins under load.

2
00:00:04.482 --> 00:00:10.354
Its main is a few lines, yet it takes a while to answer, then answers slowly for a while.

3
00:00:10.354 --> 00:00:11.872
Where does the time go?

4
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:23.169
First the launcher loads the JVM library, which creates a VM: it reserves the heap, generates its interpreter, and starts its threads.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.658
Then the classes.

6
00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:32.973
A 300-class test app loads 1,537 of them on JDK 21, with class sharing off.

7
00:00:32.973 --> 00:00:39.508
911 come from the JDK, and 325 are hidden classes made for lambdas.

8
00:00:39.508 --> 00:00:41.264
Yours are a fifth.

9
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:48.157
For each one, the JVM finds the class file, parses it into metaspace, and verifies the bytecode.

10
00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:51.729
It runs the static initializer later, on first use.

11
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:56.296
Your code starts in the interpreter, one bytecode at a time.

12
00:00:56.296 --> 00:01:03.320
The JIT turns hot methods into machine code, but only once it has profiles: counts of what the code really does.

13
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:11.723
A framework adds its own work: scanning for annotations, wiring beans, generating proxies, parsing config.

14
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:13.935
Now restart the JVM.

15
00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:16.564
How much of this work does it skip?

16
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:19.141
Almost none.

17
00:01:19.141 --> 00:01:26.556
Nothing survives the process, except a default archive of about 1,300 JDK classes, already parsed.

18
00:01:26.556 --> 00:01:28.598
Those are mapped from disk.

19
00:01:28.598 --> 00:01:30.868
Everything else is done again.

20
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:45.627
A tiny HTTP server on JDK 21: the VM is ready at 30 milliseconds, the app logs started at 103, and the first 200 arrives at 179.

21
00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:49.840
With the archive off, about 235 to 320.

22
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.228
Even then it isn't at full speed.

23
00:01:52.228 --> 00:01:56.020
The fastest code comes only after a method has run often.

24
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.439
You can watch it with -Xlog:class+load.

25
00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:06.072
Each line says where a class came from: the archive, the JDK, your jar, or the class that made it.

26
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:10.917
The rule: start-up is work the JVM repeats on every launch.

27
00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:14.716
Every class, a cold JIT, and the framework's own work.

28
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:15.689
Quick check.

29
00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:17.857
A hidden class has no class file.

30
00:02:17.857 --> 00:02:20.552
Why can its source never start with file?

31
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:27.824
Because file names the jar a class was read from, and a hidden class never had one.
